   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to birth certificates for children born in foreign countries; and registration for the adoption information registry; and repealing certain provisions of such law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "A8498",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A8498-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§4138-b, 4138-c & 4138-d, rpld & add §4138-a, rpld §4138-c sub 9, §4138-d sub 9, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8498A\n\nTITLE  OF  BILL:   An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nbirth certificates for children born in foreign countries; and registra-\ntion for  the  adoption  information  registry;  and  repealing  certain\nprovisions of such law relating thereto\n\nPurpose:    This  bill:  (1) centralizes within the Department of Health\n(DOH) procedures for the filing and issuance of birth  certificates  for\nchildren  adopted  in foreign countries; (2) cures a technical statutory\nflaw that prevents the release of identifying information to  consenting\nparties  under  the  Adoption Information Registry or the Mutual Consent\nVoluntary Adoption Registry if both birth parents  have  not  registered\nand  consented  to  such  release;  and (3) eliminates the access of the\nbiological siblings of adoptees to non-identifying information available\nthrough the registries.\n\nSummary of Provisions:  Section 1 of the bill repeals Public Health  Law\n(PHL)  § 4138-a pertaining to certificates of birth data. Section 1 also\ninserts new language to state that existing certificate  of  birth  data\nissued  under the current § 4138-a will still be valid after the section\nis repealed.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends Public  Health  Law  (P1-IL)  §  4138-b  to\nconform  the  issuance  of birth certificates for individuals adopted in\nother countries to the manner in which birth certificates are issued for\nbirths that occur in New York State. The bill provides  that  the  birth\ncertificate will be filed by the Commissioner of Health when there is no\nother birth record.(other than in the country of birth) for the individ-\nual.  The  bill requires that the adoptive parent be a   resident of New\nYork at the time of the adoption. In addition, the bill  requires  local\nregistrars to submit all supporting documentation for birth certificates\nissued  under  the  current version of PHL § 4138-b to the Department of\nHealth, which shall file a new birth certificate.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends PHL 4138-c to replace the term \"each parent\nwith \"either parent\" and clarify that  identifying  information  can  be\nprovided  about  and  to consenting parties registered with the Adoption\nInformation Registry. In addition, § 2 of the bill eliminates the abili-\nty of biological siblings to access non-identifying information  through\nthe registry.\n\nSection 4 of the bill repeals PHL § 4138-c(9).\n\nSection  5  of  the  bill  amends PHL § 4138-d to replace the term \"each\nparent\" with \"either parent\" and clarify  that  identifying  information\ncan  be  shared  among  consenting  parties  registered  with the Mutual\nConsent Voluntary Adoption Information Registry. Section 4 of  the  bill\nalso eliminates the-ability of biological siblings to access non-identi-\nfying information through such registry.\n\nSection 6 of the bill repeals PHL § 4138-d(9).\n\nSection  7  of  the  bill provides that the bill would take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nA. Foreign Adoptions\n\nDomestic  Relations  Law  §  111-c  provides  that  a  final judgment of\nadoption granted in a foreign nation  shall  have  the  same  force  and\neffect as a judgment of adoption entered by a New York court, as long as\neither  adopting  parent  is a resident of New York, and the validity of\nthe foreign adoption has been verified by the issuance of an IR-3  immi-\ngrant  visa  for  the  child  by the United States Citizenship and Immi-\ngration Services.\n\nPHL § 4137 provides that a registrar who receives a certificate of birth\nfor filing shall furnish a copy to  the.  child's  parents  or  guardian\nwithin ten days and without charge.\n\nPHL  §  4138-a  provides  that  the  Commissioner of Health will issue a\n\"Certificate of Birth Data\" when presented with proof that a person  who\nis  under  the  age of 18 and born outside of the United States has been\nvalidly adopted by residents of  this  State  pursuant  to  a  judgment,\norder, or decree of adoption issued by a court of competent jurisdiction\nin this state, another state, or a foreign country.\n\nPHL  §4138-b provides that whenever a foreign adoption recognized pursu-\nant to to Domestic Relations Law § 111-c is reported to a local  regist-\nrar,  the  registrar  shall issue a birth certificate for the child. The\nbirth certificate will be issued and filed upon receipt  of:  (1)  proof\nthat  the  adoptive  parent  or child is a resident of this state; (2) a\ncopy of the adoption documents of the jurisdiction or country  in  which\nthe  child  was  adopted;  (3)  a  certified  translation of the foreign\nadoption documents, evidence of the date and place of the child's birth;\nand (4) evidence of IR-3 immigrant visa status, or a successor immigrant\nvisa status. The birth certificate shall include the child's name,  sex,\ndate  of birth, time of birth, place of birth, mother's maiden name, and\nfather's name.\n\nPHL § 4173 authorizes a local registrar to  issue  certified  copies  or\ncertified  transcripts of birth certificates upon request of an individ-\nual, if he or she is at least 18 years old, or upon the  request  of  an\nindividual's  parent  or legal representative. Because births are regis-\ntered in the locality where they occurred pursuant to PHL § 4130, a copy\nof a birth certificate is not available from  any  locality  other  than\nwhere it occurred.\n\nPHL  §  4174  authorizes  the  Commissioner to issue certified copies or\ncertified transcripts of birth certificates upon request of an  individ-\nual,  if  he  or she is at least 18 years old, or upon the request of an\nindividual's parent or legal representative.\n\nB. Adoption Registries\n\nPHL  §  4138-c establishes the Adoption Information Registry within DOH,\npermitting an adoptee who is at least 18 years old to  access  identify-\ning. and non-identifying information about his or her biological parents\nor siblings.\n\nPHL  §  4138-d  authorizes  the  voluntary  agencies involved in private\nadoptions to establishes a Mutual Consent Voluntary  Adoption  Registry,\npermitting an adoptee who is at least 18 years old to access identifying\nand  non-identifying  information about his or her biological parents or\nsiblings.\n\nLegislative History:  This is a new proposal.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nA. Foreign Adoptions\n\nChapter 329 of the Laws of 2008 added Domestic Relations Law § 111-e and\nPHL § 4138-b to ensure that foreign adoptions are recognized in New York\nand New York birth certificates can be  obtained  by  such  adoptees  or\ntheir  parents.    While  PHL § 4138-b provides that a birth certificate\nwill be issued by a local registrar in the case of a  foreign  adoption,\nthe  statute  does  not  set  forth  any  criteria - such as an adoptive\nparent's residence at the time of adoption - that could be used to iden-\ntify which of the approximately 1,500 local registrars in New York State\nwould be authorized to issue a birth certificate in a  particular  case.\nAs  a  result,  the  statute  permits multiple registrars to issue birth\ncertificates for the same adoptee, which could be used to commit identi-\nty theft or other frauds. Moreover, the statute effectively imposes  the\nburden of reviewing foreign adoption papers upon local registrars, which\nmay not have the resources or expertise for such an undertaking.\n\nIn  addition,  the  statute does not require that birth certificates for\nforeign adoptions issued by local registrars be  filed  with  DOH.  This\nprevents  the  State  from  effectively  overseeing  or  assisting local\nregistrars in the issuance of  the  certificates.  Further,  since  such\nbirth  certificates are not filed with DOH, an adoptee Will be unable to\nobtain a copy of his or her birth certificate if he or she does not know\nwhere the original certificate was filed.\n\nIn place of the current requirement that  local  registrars  issue.  and\nfile  birth  certificates for foreign adoptions, this bill provides that\nthe Commissioner shall file birth certificates in such cases. - A certi-\nfied copy of a birth certificate  filed  by  the  Commissioner  will  be\nprovided  pursuant  to  PHL  §  4137  or,  on request, pursuant to PHL\n4174(h). Birth certificates that may have been filed by local registrars\nunder the existing version of § 4138-b will be refiled by DOH under  the\nbill.\n\nThese changes will better conform birth certificate procedures for chil-\ndren  born  in other counties and adopted by New York State residents to\nthe birth certificate procedures for children born in New York State. In\naddition. it will reduce the opportunity  for  fraud  arising  from  the\nissuance  of  multiple birth certificates. and will ensure that foreign-\nborn adopted children will be able  to  obtain  copies  of  their  birth\ncertificates  from  DOH.  Moreover,  centralizing the responsibility for\nbirth certificates for adoptees born in other countries within DOH  will\nspare  local registrars the fiscal and administrative burdens of review-\ning immigration documents and creating  and  maintaining  the  necessary\nbirth records.\n\nThe  bill  also  imposes  several  other safeguards to ensure that birth\nrecords are created only  when  appropriate,  In  particular,  the  bill\nprovides  that  the  Commissioner  shall file a birth certificate for an\nadoptee born in a foreign country only if there is no birth record other\nthan in the country of birth for  the  individual.  DOH  will  establish\npolicies  for the provision of proof that meets such standards. In addi-\ntion, the bill requires that an adoptive parent be  a  resident  of  New\nYork at the time of the adoption.\n\nFurthermore,  this bill eliminates the current law authorizing the issu-\nance of a certificate of birth data, since foreign-born adoptees will be\nable to obtain birth certificates under PHL § 4138-b.  However,  certif-\nicates  of  birth  data  issued prior to the effective date of this bill\nwill still be valid.\n\nB. Adoption Registries\n\nDOH's Adoption Information Registry permits an adoptee who is  at  least\n18  years  old  to  access  three  types of information about his or her\nbiological parents or sibling. Identifying  information,  consisting  of\nnames  and  current addresses, can be shared only if the adoptee and the\nbiological parents and siblings are all registered and have consented to\nthe release of information. Non-identifying information about the  birth\nparents,  such  as general appearance, religion, ethnicity, race, educa-\ntion, and occupation, and the facts and  circumstances  surrounding  the\nadoption  can  be  provided to an adoptee or to the adoptee's biological\nsibling, even if the birth parents do not register  or  consent  to  the\nrelease of information, Medical information can be provided by the birth\nparents  to  the  registry at any time after a child is adopted, and the\nadoptee can obtain such information if and when he or she registers.\n\nThe Mutual Consent Voluntary Adoption Registry permits private  adoption\nagencies  to  carries out the same functions as the Adoption Information\nRegistry, and permits similar access to  information  upon  registration\nand, when appropriate, consent.\n\nChapter  469  of  the  Laws  of 2007 amended PHL §§ 4138-c and 4135-d to\npermit birth parents whose parental rights had been involuntarily termi-\nnated to register with the Adoption Information Registry or  the  Mutual\nConsent  Voluntary  Adoption  Registry  and  consent  to  the release of\n\nmedical and/or identifying information to adult adoptees.  However,  the\nstatutory  revisions  needed  to  make  this  change had the inadvertent\neffect of requiring in all cases that both birth parents - if both  have\nbeen  identified  by  the  court  or  are  listed on the adoptee's birth\ncertificate - consent to the release of information.  As  a  result,  an\nadoptee registered with either registry cannot obtain information from a\nconsenting  birth  parent  unless the other birth parent has also regis-\ntered and consented.\n\nThe bill corrects this problem by replacing references  to  \"each  birth\nparent\"  with  references  to \"either birth parent\" and expressly noting\nthat identifying information about consenting registrants can be  shared\nwith other consenting registrants. This permits an adoptee, a biological\nsibling  and a birth parent to obtain identifying information about each\nother, even if another birth parent has not registered with  a  registry\nand provided consent to the release of identifying information. The bill\nrepeals  PHL  §  41  38-c(9)  and 4198-d(9), which currently permits one\nbirth parent to register  and  consent  when  another  birth  parent  is\ndeceased.  Because  the  bill permits the release of information even if\nonly one parent registers and consents, these subdivisions are no longer\nnecessary.\n\nFinally, the bill addresses  another  flaw  in  the  existing  statutory\nlanguage that allows the biological sibling of an adoptee to have access\nto  information  even  if  the adoptee is not registered. In most cases,\nthis would be innocuous, as the biological sibling, who was not  adopted\nout, is presumably aware of the identity of his or her parents. However,\nhis  or  her  parents  may  not wish the sibling to have access to other\nnon-identifying information included in the registry, such as the  facts\nand  circumstances  relating  to  the  nature and cause of the adoption.\nAccordingly, the bill eliminates the ability of biological  siblings  to\naccess  non-identifying  information,  and  permits  them access only to\nidentifying information which is shared on consent.\n\nBudget Implications:  This bill is not expected to have a fiscal  impact\nto the State.\n\nEffective Date:  This bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
